# Automation and script editing

An editor combines configuration and actions that may affect the home. People need a readable hierarchy, a stable draft and a clear account of what a test will execute. The native editor remains responsible for editing, validation, undo and traces.

**HA-CTRL-009 — reference design specification.** This specifies the local design coverage requested by HA-GAP-009 for an existing Home Assistant capability. The examples do not install a native adapter, connect to a backend or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Operation | State and consequence to expose |
| --- | --- |
| Edit draft | Unsaved changes; native editor state and undo remain available. |
| Save | Persist configuration only after the backend confirms it. Do not say the devices have changed. |
| Enable | Future matching triggers can run the automation. It is not evidence of a current execution. |
| Run or test | State exactly which actions, conditions or branch the selected native test evaluates, and which targets it can affect. |
| Disable or delete | Explain effects on future triggers and current execution from the native contract; do not promise a rollback. |
| Trace | Evidence about a particular run, with its time and branch path; it is not a general success certificate. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Separate schema errors, permission failures, save conflicts and action failures. A field or YAML validation error needs the exact location and retained text where the editor supports retention. A lost connection must not become “Saved”. Preserve the draft and native undo if actually available, and explain any recovery limitation honestly.

Nested Choose, If, Repeat and parallel actions need explicit hierarchy. Display order can affect execution; a visual rearrangement must not silently flatten branches. Offer or retain the native non-drag operation where available, and record an accessibility gap if the installed editor cannot perform a necessary reorder by keyboard.

An automation can be saved and disabled, enabled but never triggered, or currently running. Do not collapse those into one “Complete” state. A manual action test can behave differently from the trigger path; its result does not establish that every condition or trigger worked.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Use actual native labels for the version being documented. Explain required targets, modes and significant effects before a test. Keep YAML syntax and entity IDs exact and monospaced. A translated explanatory label must not alter a code value. Separate the error's supplied cause from suggested investigation; do not blame a device without trace evidence.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Use real headings and nested lists for explanatory outlines. In the editor, preserve labelled action blocks, keyboard selection, focus after move/delete and accessible error navigation. Dense code surfaces retain alignment and native scaling; do not force 19px prose styling into the code editor. Keep Run visually and semantically separate from Save.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Hand editing to the native visual or YAML editor under Settings > Automations & scenes. Theme variables do not provide an editor model, undo stack or trace engine. These fixed forms demonstrate content and error placement only. A future scoped adapter must leave native dirty state, validation, ordering and callbacks intact and must document the frontend version it tests.

